{
  "image_name": "a32c21565b146c0348ceaf46402d555c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two photoelectrochemical cell designs for driving reactions in an electrolyte under illumination and applied bias. In both, light hits semiconductor electrodes immersed in solution, and an external circuit moves electrons between the illuminated electrode and a counterelectrode.\n\nThe left schematic shows a simpler arrangement based on a single semiconductor on ITO with surface catalysts. A potential gradient across the interface separates charges, and electrons accumulated in the semiconductor are used for reduction reactions at catalyst sites in the electrolyte. The bias and counterelectrode complete the circuit.\n\nThe right schematic shows a fuller tandem-like photoelectrochemical scheme with a photoanode on one side and a photocathode on the other. Band positions are indicated by CB and VB, and light generates electrons and holes that drive complementary reactions. At the photoanode, holes oxidize water to oxygen and protons, while electrons are extracted through the external circuit. At the photocathode, incoming electrons participate in reduction of carbon dioxide and protons to products such as CH4 and CH3OH. Overall, the figure highlights how combining photoanode and photocathode functions enables coupled oxidation and reduction, converting light and electrical bias into chemical fuels.",
